From 0d70e02f3e2c551943ff893601cddc9eb3cef388 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tyler Warburton <tyler@cipherplay.net>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 17:44:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] vrf@1.0: stateless BLS beacon verification and seed
 derivation

A new preloaded device that a Lua process can call from inside its
computation (ao.resolve) or over HTTP, so a randomness beacon's round
can be checked on-node, deterministically, by every node replaying it.

- verify-bls: BLS12-381 "minimal-pubkey-size" (keys G1, sigs G2,
  DST BLS_SIG_BLS12381G2_XMD:SHA-256_SSWU_RO_NUL_). Returns valid and,
  when valid, value = hex(SHA-256("round|" ++ hex(sig))), the beacon's
  round value. Malformed input gives valid=false with a reason.
- derive: hex of SHA-256(seed ++ decimal(counter+k)) blocks, <= 1024 bytes.

Verification uses supranational/blst v0.3.16 (submodule native/lib/blst,
built with __BLST_PORTABLE__) through native/blst_nif, built by a rebar
pre_hook like secp256k1 and run on a dirty CPU scheduler. Both points are
subgroup-checked and the identity key is refused before the pairing.

Tests: interop vectors generated with the beacon's own noble-curves code
(2-of-3 DKG group sig, single-key sig, wrong message/key, corrupted,
non-subgroup points, identity, garbage), round 1 of the live beacon with
its published value, derive, malformed inputs, and a lua@5.3a module
calling the device. scripts/vrf-test.sh builds the tree in erlang:27
and runs rebar3 device test --devices dev_vrf,dev_lua.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
