From 47ca8cbb73365098a3162ce2d84ff39859f3f8f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tyler Warburton <tyler@cipherplay.net>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:20:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] timelock@1.0: time-lock reveal/crack checks; rename from
 vrf@1.0

The device's main job is now checking the timelock tier's puzzle answers
(Random-Process SPEC 2.3-2.5, section 3), so it is renamed vrf@1.0 ->
timelock@1.0 (dev_timelock). verify-bls and derive are unchanged.

New keys, stateless and pure; bad input gives valid=false with a reason:
- derive-x: x = (int(expand(seed, bits(N)+64)) mod N)^2 mod N.
- verify-reveal: 1 < p < N, N rem p = 0, y = x^(2^t mod (p-1)(q-1)) mod N.
- verify-crack: Wesolowski, proof^l * x^(2^t mod l) = y (mod N), with
  0 < y, proof < N; l = nextPrime over SHA-256("rng/wesolowski|N|x|y|t"),
  trial division below 1000 plus Miller-Rabin on the first 20 primes.
Both verify keys also return x. Hex is lowercase without leading zeros
(BigInt.toString(16)); other spellings are refused as non-canonical, since
the challenge hashes the hex as given. Caps: 4096-bit moduli and hex
inputs, 4096-byte seeds, t <= 2^32. Erlang bignums and crypto:mod_pow.

Tests: vectors from the JS reference (provider/timelock.mjs): 1024/2048-bit
moduli, t = 1024/4096, valid reveals with p and q, wrong y/p/seed/t, p = 1,
p = N, valid and corrupted cracks, out-of-range values, garbage hex, and a
2048-bit t = 2^27 reveal and crack (proof made with the trapdoor); challenge
primes match the reference's. A lua@5.3a process calls verify-reveal and
verify-crack through ao.resolve. scripts/vrf-test.sh -> timelock-test.sh.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
